/*!
    \page creator-reference-terminal-view.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-use

    \title Open a terminal

    To open the \uicontrol Terminal view:

    \list
        \li Go to \uicontrol View > \uicontrol Output, and then select
            \uicontrol Terminal.
        \li Select the \uicontrol Terminal output view.
        \li Select \key {Alt+5} (or \key {Cmd+5} on \macos).
        \li Select \uicontrol {Run in Terminal} in \uicontrol Projects >
            \uicontrol {Run Settings} for an application, and then run the
            application.
        \li Select the \uicontrol {Open Terminal} button in \uicontrol Projects
            > \uicontrol {Build Settings} > \uicontrol {Build Environment}.
    \endlist

    \image {qtcreator-output-terminal.webp} {Terminal}

    \section1 View output in the terminal

    \list
        \li To select a word in a terminal, double-click it.

        \li To select the whole line, triple-click it.

        \li To select all text, select \uicontrol {Select All}
            in the context menu or select \key {Ctrl+A}.

        \li To open links in a browser, files in the editor, or folders in the
            \l {File System} view, hold \key Ctrl (or \key Cmd on \macos) and
            select them. To copy the target of a link instead, hold
            \key {Ctrl+Shift} (or \key {Cmd+Shift} on \macos).

            Programs can also mark text as a link. For example, GCC links the
            warning options in its output to its manual when you pass
            \c {-fdiagnostics-urls=always} to it.

        \li To \l{Search in current file}{search} through the output, select
            \key {Ctrl+F}.
    \endlist

    \section1 View pictures in the terminal

    Programs can print pictures into the terminal in the sixel format, for
    example \c img2sixel or \c {chafa -f sixels}.

    On Windows, the console host that comes with Windows passes on only the
    text. To view the pictures, go to \preferences > \uicontrol Terminal >
    \uicontrol {Console Host}, and select \uicontrol {Download...} to download
    a newer console host from Microsoft. Then open a new terminal.

    \section1 Paste text

    If the text that you paste has control characters, such as line breaks,
    and the program running in the terminal did not announce that it can
    handle them, \QC asks before pasting the text. Such a paste can run
    commands that the text only appears to contain. To paste without asking,
    go to \preferences > \uicontrol Terminal, and clear
    \uicontrol {Confirm pasting control characters}.

    Programs can replace the contents of the clipboard only if you select
    \uicontrol {Allow programs to set the clipboard}.

    \section1 Run \QC commands from the terminal

    To use the \c qtc command-line options in the terminal, enter:

    \code [shell]
    qtc [option] [filename[:line_number[:column_number]]]
    \endcode

    To view the options, enter:

    \code [shell]
    qtc -help
    \endcode

    \section1 Insert \QC variables

    To insert expanded values of \QC variables into the terminal, select them
    in the \uicontrol {Insert Macro Variable} list.

    \section1 Set keyboard shortcut preferences

    By default, the \QC keyboard shortcuts take precedence in the terminal. To
    keep them from interfering in the terminal, select
    \inlineimage {icons/keyboardlock.png} {Block shortcuts in terminal}
    (\uicontrol {Block shortcuts in terminal}). The keyboard shortcuts for
    opening preferences, finding text, maximizing the output view, and opening
    and switching terminals still work.

    To send the escape key to the terminal instead of closing the terminal,
    press \key {Shift+Esc}. To always send it, select \uicontrol {Shift+Esc}
    on the toolbar, or go to \preferences > \uicontrol Terminal, and select
    \uicontrol {Send escape key to terminal}.

    \image {qtcreator-preferences-terminal.webp} {Terminal preferences}

    \section1 Run terminal in a separate window

    To open the terminal in a separate window, go to \preferences >
    \uicontrol Terminal, and clear \uicontrol {Use internal terminal}.

    \section1 Use system terminal on Linux and \macos

    On Linux and \macos, you can set the terminal to open by selecting
    \preferences > \uicontrol Environment > \uicontrol System.

    \sa {Use \QC variables}, {View output}, {Command-line options},
    {Keyboard shortcuts}
*/
